  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nA cold front has pushed through western and most of central ND\nas of just before 4AM CDT. Behind the cold front, winds have\nbeen gusting to 35 to 45 mph, with an occasional gust to 45-50 \nmph. The fall-rise couplet has passed but pressure rises, strong\ngradient winds and cold advection will continue through 12-15 \nUTC before dropping off late morning and through the afternoon. \nMay have to monitor the winds over eastern portions of the \nforecast area this morning to see if we would possibly need to \nextend the advisory for a while, if so, think it would be in the\nnorth central rather than the JRV.\n\nLight post-frontal precipitation in northeast Montana is quickly\ndrying up as it pushes east. If there is any measurable\nprecipitation in the northwest it will be done by 12 UTC. Strong\nsubsidence behind the front will also continue to erode the \nlower cloud deck behind the cold front. Considerable clouds \nearly this morning will be on the decrease with mostly sunny to \npartly cloudy skies expected by this afternoon. Highs are \nexpected to range from the mid 40s to mid 50s.\n\nHigh pressure will build over the forecast area tonight with\nlows mainly in the 20s, with some readings in the teens\npossible.\n\nOn Sunday, a closed upper low over the Desert Southwest begins\nlifting northward. Mid and upper level moisture with this low\napproaches southern ND by late in the day, but precipitation is\nexpected to stay shunted south of the forecast area. There could\nbe some light precipitation slipping into the far south Sunday\nnight, but most if not all should remain south of the border.\nAnother cool day is expected Sunday with highs in the lower 40s\nto lower 50s. Lows Sunday night will be mainly in the 20s to\nlower 30s.\n\nThe aforementioned closed upper low finally lifts through the\ncentral and into the northern Plains Monday through Tuesday. On\nMonday, light precipitation should remain along and south of \nthe I-94 corridor through the morning, with precipitation \nlifting northward through the forecast area Monday afternoon\nthrough Tuesday morning. \n\nMedium range guidance has been going back and forth as whether\nto lift this system into ND or keep it south. Currently there is\nrelatively good agreement on the upper level system lifting \ninto North Dakota Monday night. Although qpf amounts are not \noverly impressive, and they do diminish as they lift northward \ninto blocking high pressure situated over Canada, there is a \ndecent signal for some beneficial liquid with this system. \n\nThe NBM 24 hour probability of at least a quarter of precip \nending at 12Z Tuesday ranges from around 30 percent from  \nHettinger to Bismarck and Carrington, up to around 80 percent in\nthe southern James River Valley (Dickey and LaMoure Counties). \nWith the current timing, a good portion of this precip would \nlift through the area Monday night into Tuesday morning. \nPrecipitation should be rain to begin with on Monday, but as we\ncool off Monday night, we could definitely see a transition to \na mix of rain and snow, then possibly all snow by early Tuesday \nmorning. The NBM is showing some low to moderate probabilities \n(20 to 60%) of an inch or more of snow over portions of \nsouthwest and south central ND, during this same timeframe\n(ending 12Z Tuesday). The highest probabilities are centered \nsouth and west of Bismarck along the SD border. \n\nThere is another quick moving system that approaches from the \nwest around mid-week. This system may also bring a chance of \nlight rain and snow to the forecast area.",
